    10	Model checking has been used traditionally for finding violations of temporal properties. Recently, testing or
    11	fuzzing approaches have also been applied to software systems to find temporal property violations. However,
    12	model checking suffers from state explosion, while fuzzing can only partially cover program paths. Moreover,
    13	once a violation is found, the fix for the temporal error is usually manual. In this work, we develop the first
    14	compositional static analyzer for temporal properties, and the analyzer supports a proof-based repair strategy
    15	to fix temporal bugs automatically. To enable a more flexible specification style for temporal properties, on
    16	top of the classic pre/post-conditions, we allow users to write a future-condition to modularly express the
    17	expected behaviors after the function call. Instead of requiring users to write specifications for each procedure,
    18	our approach automatically infers the procedure’s specification according to user-supplied specifications for
    19	a small number of primitive APIs. We further devise a term rewriting system to check the actual behaviors
    20	against its inferred specification. Our method supports the analysis of 1) memory usage bugs, 2) unchecked
    21	return values, 3) resource leaks, etc., with annotated specifications for 17 primitive APIs, and detects 515
    22	vulnerabilities from over 1 million lines of code ranging from ten real-world C projects. Intuitively, the benefit
    23	of our approach is that a small set of properties can be specified once and used to analyze/repair a large
    24	number of programs. Experimental results show that our tool, ProveNFix, detects 72.2% more true alarms
    25	than the latest release of the Infer static analyzer. Moreover, we show the effectiveness of our repair strategy
    26	when compared to other state-of-the-art systems — fixing 5% more memory leaks than SAVER, 40% more
    27	resource leaks than FootPatch, and with a 90% fix rate for null pointer dereferences.
