   233	The above reasoning rule for function calls captures the essence of having future-conditions,
   234	where proof obligations are highlighted . Traditional Hoare-style forward reasoning rule for
   235	function calls works roughly as follows: it retrieves the callee 푛푚 ’s spec from the environment
   236	E and if the current program state Φ entails the callee’s instantiated precondition [푦 [∗] /푥 [∗] ] Φ pre, it
   237	obtains the instantiated postcondition to be the extended program state, i.e., Φ post [′] [. Now, having]
   238	the future spec, we extend the rule with one more proof obligation: the behavior of ‘e’, i.e., Φ 푒,
   239	entails the callee’s instantiated future-condition. Each proof obligation enforces constraints for
   240	code segments, here for the code before the current call and for the code after the call, i.e., ‘e’.
   241	
   242	
   243	**2.2** **Specification Inference and Interprocedural Analysis**
   244	
   245	
   246	Many existing tools [ 24 ] [ 17 ] perform program analysis via the call-strings technique [ 35 ], which
   247	blends interprocedural flow analysis with the analysis of intraprocedural flow, turning a whole
   248	program into a single flow graph. In this way, they split the interprocedural analysis into a preanalysis phase, which gathers overestimated information about each procedure and followed by a
   249	global intraprocedural analysis. We decide to target a more accurate analysis by viewing procedures
   250	as collections of structured blocks and aim to establish input-output relations via the pre/post/future
   251	specs – where procedures can be replaced by their verified properties. This approach relates closely
   252	to most of the known techniques for program verification, and has the advantage of being rather
   253	simple and potentially admitting efficient and scalable implementations.
